From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: competition and intellectual property
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Betsy asks, essentially, what if I'm a little guy, I have an idea, and
without patent protection I get crushed by a big competitor as soon as
its obvious that the idea is profitable.

The answer is, too bad.

There are many "good ideas". For instance, the idea of starting a fast
food restaurant. Should someone out there, however, have been able to
get a patent on it? The idea of runing a discount brokerage house was
pretty good. Prehaps the inventor of that notion should have gotten
coercive protection to stop others from competing with them? 

If your idea is so trivial that anyone can just duplicate it without
effort, and you have no other competitive advantage, you DESERVE to be
crushed. Period.

This guy who got a patent on the intermitent windshield wiper, for
instance, is in my opinion a really good example. He's made several
hundred million dollars thus far, and for what? For getting the
entirely obvious idea that you could use a simple R/C circuit (one
resistor, one capacitor -- simplest timing mechanism you can imagine)
to control when your windshield wiper blades go off, and because the
auto manufacturers decided that it wasn't worth paying him to tell
them the obvious. 

Similarly, take an idea I had a while back. I figured you could make a
decent profit at running a big public access unix system for profit in
New York, especially with eight million people a local call away.
Well, I didn't have the money to do it. Someone else, Alexis Rosen,
actually ended up doing this by starting Panix a while back. Alexis is
a fairly smart guy, and Panix is pretty good. Lets say I could have
gotten "intellectual property rights" on this entirely obvious idea.
Alexis Rosen would then have had to pay me for no good reason.

Am I complaining that I missed my chance? Hell no. It was my own
fault. I didn't have all the pieces in place at once. So what? Big
deal.

I can see that the market would naturally support contractual
agreements designed to restrict access to materials we would consider
"copyrighted". I can see how the market could offer trade secret
protection. However, ideas are ideas are ideas. If your ideas are
independantly derived by someone else, I see no reason you have a
"right" to claim them as property.

Another poster has recently asked "how are patents different from
deeds to land? Its just another kind of property. If I get an idea,
why shouldn't I be able to own it?" The reason is simple. First, as a
reducto ad absurdum, should I be able to own the idea of you walking
across your front lawn to open your mailbox? Lets say I see your piece
of land, no house has been built on it, and I register the notion of
building a house on it. Should you then have to pay me royalties when
you buy the land and build on it? After all, why should that "idea" be
different from any other kind of property?

Ideas are intangible. Thousands of people can have them simultaneously
without the first person knowing or necessarily caring that others are
having them, too. Patents are a method used by companies to try to
restrain trade because they have difficulty competing on their own
merits. Merely having know-how doesn't mean you deserve a buck.
The capacity to take an idea to market and make it good enough that
the customers actually want it, along with the capacity to get the
capital needed to execute the plan, is just as important.

Perry

