Date: Tue, 2 Feb 1993 13:04:23 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: ECON: Are Medicare Expenditures Too High?

Dani Eder:
> A simple adjustment to health insurance policies and medicare would
> restore consumer sensitivity to price... 

This scheme is quite an improvement over the current system, but 
another factor to consider is that annual visits to the doctor (or 
more often for high-risk people) reduce the risk and cost of many 
kinds of cancer and heart disease, via early detection and prevention.  
This scheme discourages routine check-ups (by deducting 100% of their 
cost) while encouraging major medical expenses (by deducting only 5%).

One of the attractions of HMOs is that they pay for these yearly
checkups and encourge prevention, but their drawback is they limit
patient choice of physician.

I suggest a policy that would give the patient a $100 premium discount 
for getting an annual checkup.  The patient can choose the physician
but the checkup must be fairly standard (physical, blood test,
checks for various kinds of cancer, etc.)  High-risk patients would
get higher discounts for semi-annual checkups, but would also be
charged a higher premium for the risk.

Insurance companies should be able to discriminate in various
ways, beyond the narrow methods currently used because of tight
legal restrictions:

* Discounts to those with high serum levels of antioxidants, which are 
  inversely correlated with many kinds of cancer, lung disease, etc.
  (cf. Basel study).
* Discounts based on status of immune system & other measurable
  biological states correlating with lower health risk.
* Discounts to those who can demonstrate a low-risk genetic history.
* Discounts for those who don't smoke (as indicated by blood test).
* etc.

Subsidizing the unhealthy should be a matter for charity, especially
considering that many diseases (lung and stomach cancer, AIDS, etc.) 
are largely preventable.

As a young person at low risk of the major killers (cancer, heart
disease, AIDS) I probably subsidize our health-care system by a factor 
of 3 or more through our "private" insurance industry.  I'd love to find 
a provider that charges a fair price to healthy, low-risk customers.  
If an insurance company can conduct trustworthy, private transactions 
with its clients over national borders, at a cost lower than the savings
from restoring market equilibrium, this service can be profitable.
This is another example of metastate arbitrage: profiting from
state-caused market distortions by providing a service that works around
them.

