> Me: 
> > What about Wolf's-Head Unlimited, which specializes in protecting the 
> > outlaws of the world? 

Perry Metzger:
> Being itself outlawed, its likely that a smart guy with an F15 could collect
> millions in bounties with a single bombing run on their headquarters, and
> no one will notice.

That's unlikely, since:

(a) Unlimited's "Head-Quarters" is a virtual cryptoTAZ, not a
physical location. 
(b) The F-15 could cause collateral damage, making the bounty
hunter himself an outlaw.
(c) Wolf's-Head has signed mutual protection agreements with
insurance fraud companies, illegal-drug-traders, armed
robbery syndicates, and other outlawed organizations.  Those
"outlawed" aren't going to go without a fight, especially
not if being an "outlaw" is profitable.
(d) most criminals are not worth millions in bounty; those that
are can afford Wolf's-Head's high-end protection services (retribution
and hostage insurance). 
(e) Since Wolf's-Head is serving a profitable market niche, eliminating
it just means its competitors will flourish; it doesn't get rid of
the problem.

Universally mutual contract-signing for purposes of outlawing
or extradition may be unworkable in its current proposed form.  Would 
a drug-freindly PPL sign a contract obliging it to extradite those who 
run drugs in a "drug-free" PPL?  Would a porno PPL sign a contract 
obliging it to extradite those who sell X-rated videos under a Mormon PPL?  
Would a skeptical PPL accept the judgement of a PPL that allowed the 
testimony of psychics?  If Salmon Rushdie had signed up for an
Ayatollah's PPL and then written his book, would we be obliged to
outlaw him or his protection service?  In general, PPL's with sharp 
ethical or procedural differences are not likely to sign broad extradition 
agreements.  Even if there is something every PPL can agree is a crime (eg 
scientifically proven armed robbery), this opens up a market niche for 
a service to protect profitable crime of that type.

Nick Szabo				szabo@techbook.com

