From: CAROTH@delphi.com
Subject: ECON: Are Medicare Expenditures Too High?

The government in Germany does not control the prices at all. The federal
health insurance says that if you are sick and need medication, they will
pay in full the price of the CHEAPEST medication of that kind (e.g. generic
stuff rather than brandnames). You may choose to want a more expensive
product in which case you would have to pay the difference between the 
amount of the cheapest product of that kind avaible and the product you want.
So if the producers of medicine price their products reasonably, so that the
federal health insurance pays for it, they make more money, because they sell
more than if they make their product more expensive, and less people will use
it, because the federal health insurance does not pay it in full.

The same thing is true for doctors. You can go to any doctor you want, but if
he charges more than average, you may have to pay the difference between 
acerage and what he charged you. In dental care the health insurance started
to make you pay 10% or more (depending how often you visited your dentist in
the last couple years, what kind of materials he used, etc.) about 3 years
ago.

