From: davidc@cs.uwa.edu.au (David Cake)
Subject: RIGHTS:Patents

> 
> 
> Perry's discussions of patents is, in my opinion, biased. IMO, there
> are patentable inventions, and I would naturally expect some form
> of mediation to develop (Patent Office, or private inventions insurance
> agency) which would determine if inventions are patentable. 

Remember why patents were created, to encourage the sharing of ideas that were
originally trade secrets. If you remove patent law, the 'patentable inventions'
fall into three classes.
a) those that it is easy to independently invent, which shouldn't be patentable
anyway, really. Unfortunately many software patents most definately fall into
this category.
b) those that can be protected by trade secrets law (which can quite easily
be handled by civil law/PPL) which gives one campany a monopoly ( at least until
the product is reverse engineered). Wether the general community is better or
worse of (and wether the company is better or worse off) depends on how
difficult it is to reverse engineer. Rich pickings for lawyers, I feel.
c) those few inventions that are difficult to invent, but easily understandable
once invented (like safety pins). The inventors of this sort of thing will
lose out without patents, but they can still make a lot of money, with lots of
capital behind them to establish market share.
	A system without patents is certainly workable. Wether it is a better
system than now, I don't know. It may well be tha patents could work in a PPL
as a contract - the PPL agrees to grant some preferential manufacturing rights,
in return for knowledge of the manufacture process. Other people that don't 
honour the patent, are not given acess to the PPL patent library, and perhaps 
other sanctions as well.
	This has two big problems.
	It makes the PPL seem alarmingly nation-stateish (PPLs shouldn't have
many manufacturing controls) and it is probably completely unworkable, as 
information wants to free (or is easy to disemminate once you have it).

